WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.260
OK.

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:04.520
Let's go next to our caller from the four
four three.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.400
Area code, who is calling today from four
four three.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:08.830
Hey, it's me.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:10.260
Yes, it is.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:11.260
Who's this?

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:14.080
Hi, this is Alex from Maryland.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.610
How you doing, David?

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:16.610
Good.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:17.610
How are you?

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:18.610
I'm doing good.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:19.970
I just had a quick question for you.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:25.360
If you were to update the federal poverty
levels, how would you do it in a progressive

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:26.360
way?

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:27.360
That's interesting.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.910
So there's a couple different things.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:36.710
The federal poverty level in and of itself
is is potentially not a good measure because

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:40.219
cost of living varies so dramatically from
state to state.

00:00:40.219 --> 00:00:46.449
So federal poverty level that has to apply
to New York City and rural Wyoming is inherently

00:00:46.449 --> 00:00:50.250
problematic because the cost of living is
so dramatically different in those two places.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:51.770
You know what I mean?

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:52.969
Yeah, yeah, definitely.

00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:59.089
And so so first and foremost, I would say
we would want to maybe revisit what the federal

00:00:59.089 --> 00:01:03.539
poverty level is even used for, because it's
not a good measure for a lot of different

00:01:03.539 --> 00:01:04.539
things.

00:01:04.539 --> 00:01:07.530
Maybe maybe it should be done at the state
level or county or city that, you know, the

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:12.870
problem about city is do you do it based on
where someone lives or where someone works?

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:16.290
You have people who who work in New York City
but live in Connecticut.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:17.290
There's complexities.

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:25.970
But conceptually, I. I think that what it
costs to the living wage needs to be taken

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:27.730
into account.

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:34.030
When we think of the federal poverty level
and probably the poverty level, combined with

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:40.500
whatever social safety net benefits you would
qualify for, should add together to be a living

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.500
wage.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.970
Like the ideal thing would be every job pays
a living wage.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:51.370
That's a different type of legislative apparatus
then setting the poverty level.

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:57.130
So I think that we should be looking at a
framework where, adjusted for cost of living

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:06.200
poverty level, has to get you a certain amount
of social safety net benefits where when combined,

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.009
you get to a living wage.

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:11.159
If that makes sense and if this is like a
fantasy, I mean, this is like we're nowhere

00:02:11.159 --> 00:02:12.719
even close to this being a serious discussion.

00:02:12.719 --> 00:02:16.530
Yeah, this is all theoretical at this point.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:21.090
I was just looking up apartments around my
area and I was I saw that the federal poverty

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:25.090
level is still fifteen thousand dollars and
change, and I thought that was ridiculous.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:28.269
There's no way you could live anywhere in
the U.S. on that.

00:02:28.269 --> 00:02:29.269
So.

00:02:29.269 --> 00:02:30.269
Right.

00:02:30.269 --> 00:02:33.810
Good question is that if you know, if it's
fifteen thousand in a lot of places, you could

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.239
be making double that.

00:02:35.239 --> 00:02:36.980
And you can't afford a one bedroom apartment.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:38.670
And that makes no sense.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.349
Which I think is the point I'm making.

00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:41.349
Yes, exactly.

00:02:41.349 --> 00:02:43.049
All right, David, thanks.

00:02:43.049 --> 00:02:44.049
OK.

00:02:44.049 --> 00:02:45.049
Thank you.

00:02:45.049 --> 00:02:46.049
Appreciate the call.

00:02:46.049 --> 00:02:47.049
Yeah, likewise.

00:02:47.049 --> 00:02:49.519
Let me go next to our caller from the three,
four seven area code.

00:02:49.519 --> 00:02:51.180
Who's calling today from three four seven.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.640
Oh, lost my pen here.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:53.810
Hey, let me.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:54.980
Yes, it is.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:56.930
Hey, Dave, how are you?

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:57.930
Good.

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:58.930
New York.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:02:59.930
Hey, Peter, what's up?

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:05.620
I was wondering if you could wave a magic
wand and implement any three policies.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:06.620
What would it do?

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:07.620
Oh, boy.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:14.650
I mean, I guess at the micro level, some kind
of healthcare for all would be would be number

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:15.810
one.

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:23.180
Number two, we would want to do campaign finance
reform because that connects to.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:29.900
Like, if you care about education or abortion
or whatever, the way that elections are financed

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:36.379
is a huge part of how all of those issues
are dealt with and used as fundraising wedges.

00:03:36.379 --> 00:03:38.680
So we would we would do campaign finance reform.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:39.769
And that's not one law.

00:03:39.769 --> 00:03:40.769
Right.

00:03:40.769 --> 00:03:42.299
Like, that's changing a huge apparatus.

00:03:42.299 --> 00:03:48.290
I would say health care, campaign finance
and probably something serious on climate

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:52.459
change, because, again, it's going to be hard
to deal with any other issues if the country's

00:03:52.459 --> 00:03:54.709
under water or nobody can breathe.

00:03:54.709 --> 00:03:57.599
So maybe most urgently, I would go with those
three.

00:03:57.599 --> 00:04:01.189
Well, what do you feel about rank choice voting?

00:04:01.189 --> 00:04:08.069
Yeah, rank it so that if you said top five,
I would include changing the voting system.

00:04:08.069 --> 00:04:10.140
And I've actually said before I do.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:14.360
I'm not necessarily married to ranked choice,
but there's a few different versions, including

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:17.959
single transferable vote and a number of others
that we've talked about.

00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:21.250
What if you said, what's your election reform
package?

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:26.160
It would be a combination of getting rid of
first past the post combined with campaign

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.160
finance reform.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:30.500
And I've got a bunch of clips about ranked
choice versus other systems that you can check

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:31.500
out.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:32.500
I remember all of that.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:33.500
Yeah.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:34.500
All right.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:35.500
Thanks a lot.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:36.500
OK.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:37.500
Appreciate it.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:38.500
Thank you so much.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:39.880
We're going to take a quick break, but we
will go right back to the phones when we get

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:40.880
back.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.160
So if you are on hold, don't hang up, OK?

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:44.160
Don't hang up.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:45.160
You've already called in.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:46.160
You're already on hold.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.660
And we will get some more calls right after
this short break.

